"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_core_en"][12]={"university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M12-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M12-L1 – Model Boundaries, Quantities and Physical Domains<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M12 – Bachelor: Modelling and Simulation of Complex Systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Physical\nbalances<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Differential\nequations<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M5&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Systems<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Define the boundary of a\nphysical multi-domain system, identify states and interfaces, and\nformulate a consistent energy balance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From the engineering question to the model boundary<\/h2>\n<p>A model is a purpose-specific abstraction, not a complete copy of an\nobject. To determine the speed a motor reaches under a given load, an\nelectromechanical model is often sufficient. To assess heating over two\nminutes, at least one thermal state must also be included. For the\nspatial distribution of winding temperature, however, a single\ntemperature value would often be too coarse. Before choosing the\nequations, specify the quantities of interest, time interval, inputs,\ndesired accuracy and permissible simplifications.<\/p>\n<p>Consider an idealised permanent-magnet DC motor. The supply and\ndriven machine are outside the model boundary; the rotor, winding and\nits lumped thermal mass are inside. For simplicity, frictional heat is\nreleased directly into the environment; brush losses, magnetic\nsaturation and separate housing temperatures are omitted. The model\nboundary determines whether an energy flow appears as an internal\nexchange or an external outflow.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_037_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Electrical, mechanical and thermal model boundaries, power flows and stored quantities.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: The motor energy balance distinguishes magnetic,\nkinetic and thermal storage; internal electromechanical transfers cancel\nin the total balance.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical domains and power quantities<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Domain<\/th>\n<th>Pair of quantities<\/th>\n<th>Instantaneous power<\/th>\n<th>Storage in the model<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Electrical<\/td>\n<td>Voltage\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>u<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ncurrent\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>i<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ui<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><msup><mi>i<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Li^2/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mechanical, translation<\/td>\n<td>Force\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nvelocity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Fv<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">mv^2/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mechanical, rotation<\/td>\n<td>Torque\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nangular velocity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ω<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><msup><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J\\omega^2/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Thermal<\/td>\n<td>Temperature\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nheat flow rate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>Q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>Q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C(T-T_a)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrelative to the environment<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The products of the first three pairs of quantities yield watts. In\nconventional heat-transfer modelling, by contrast, the heat flow rate is\nalready a power;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mover><mi>Q<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T\\dot Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwould not be a meaningful power expression. A thermodynamic port\nrepresentation can use temperature and entropy flow rate; this is a\ndifferent modelling convention. We use a constant thermal storage\ncoefficient\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin J/K.<\/p>\n<p>A signal port carries an information value (for example, the measured\nspeed as an input to a controller). It is not automatically a physical\npower port. For ideal physical connections, common potential quantities\nmust be compatible and the algebraic sum of the flow quantities directed\ntowards the connection point must be zero. A model must apply this\nconvention consistently in all components.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>States, parameters and algebraic quantities<\/h2>\n<p>A state carries the system history necessary for subsequent\nevolution. In the chosen motor, these states are winding current\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>i<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nangular velocity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ω<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand temperature rise\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta=T-T_a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe temperature difference in K is sufficient. If position is also to be\ntracked,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>φ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot\\varphi=\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis required as an additional state equation; this is unnecessary for the\nspeed question.<\/p>\n<p>Parameters include inductance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nmoment of inertia\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>J<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthermal capacitance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand thermal conductance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>G<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin W/K. Winding resistance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R(\\theta)=R_0(1+\\alpha\\theta)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis an algebraic function of the state in the model, not an independent\nstorage element. Input\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand load\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M_L(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare prescribed. Outputs can be calculated from states and inputs, for\nexample\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_\\mathrm{in}=ui<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor winding temperature\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_a+\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>The energy relative to the reference level is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mi>L<\/mi><msup><mi>i<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mi>J<\/mi><msup><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=\\frac12 Li^2+\\frac12 J\\omega^2+C\\theta.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas the viscous friction torque, the verifiable balance is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>E<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><msup><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot E=ui-M_L\\omega-b\\omega^2-G\\theta.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The Joule losses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><msup><mi>i<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Ri^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare an internal transfer to the thermal store here; they must not also\nbe subtracted as an external outflow. By contrast, with a boundary\nenclosing only the electrical and mechanical parts, they would have to\nappear as an outflow. The sign\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-G\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalso allows heat input from the environment when the temperature\ndifference is negative.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and model selection<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nH,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.02<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J=0.02<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkg m²,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=40<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nJ/K,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nA,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>30<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=30<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrad/s and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta=20<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nK, the magnetic energy is 1.25 J, the kinetic energy 9 J and the thermal\nenergy relative to the environment 800 J. Total: 810.25 J. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>24<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=24<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M_L=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nNm,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.02<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=0.02<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nNm s/rad and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nW/K,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>E<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>120<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>15<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>18<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>47<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot E=120-15-18-40=47\\,\\mathrm W.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The positive balance does not mean that all states increase:\nmechanical and thermal stores may release or absorb energy\nsimultaneously. The timescales differ; a large amount of stored energy\nis not the same as high instantaneous power. The complete dynamic model\nfollows in the next lesson.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A 200 µF capacitor is at 24 V. Determine the stored energy.<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.0576<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Cu^2/2=0.0576<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nJ.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A thermal store with 40 J/K receives a net 30 W. How quickly is its\ntemperature changing at that instant?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>T<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>30<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>40<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.75<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot T=30/40=0.75<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nK/s.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Is the motor model intended to predict the highest temperature\nwithin a winding?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> The single-node model provides only a\nrepresentative temperature; spatial gradients require additional thermal\nnodes or a field model.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Further reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Coupled\nmodels and state space<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://doc.modelica.org/om/Modelica.UsersGuide.Overview.html\">Modelica\nStandard Library – Overview of physical domains<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Library overview:\nanalog electrical components and machines; further reading. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://doc.modelica.org/Modelica%204.0.0/Resources/helpWSM/Modelica/Modelica.Thermal.HeatTransfer.Examples.Motor.html\">Modelica\n– Second-order thermal motor model<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Information: second-order motor\nthermal model with losses and heat capacities. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M12-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M12-L2 – Coupled Models and State Space<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M12 – Bachelor: Modelling and Simulation of Complex Systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Model\nboundaries<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Matrices<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Feedback<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Develop a nonlinear state-space\nmodel from energy and force balances and determine its local\nlinearisation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three coupled storage equations<\/h2>\n<p>The electrical loop includes the back EMF\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe temperature-dependent voltage drop\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R(\\theta)i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the inductance. The electromagnetic torque\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ki<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nfriction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand prescribed load torque\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M_L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nact on the rotor. In consistent SI units, the ideal electromechanical\ncoupling has the same numerical value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor both relationships\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{V\\,s/rad}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand Nm/A, respectively). This follows from lossless conversion\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k\\omega i=ki\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mover><mi>i<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L\\dot i=u-R(\\theta)i-k\\omega,<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mover><mi>ω<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J\\dot\\omega=ki-b\\omega-M_L,<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mover><mi>θ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mi>i<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C\\dot\\theta=R(\\theta)i^2-G\\theta,\\qquad R(\\theta)=R_0(1+\\alpha\\theta).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Heat is produced in the winding; frictional heat is an external\noutflow, as in <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M12-L1<\/a>.\nThe model applies only over a temperature range where resistance is\npositive and can be described approximately by a linear relationship. It\nassumes a constant magnetic field, a constant ambient temperature and an\nideal voltage source.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_038_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Coupled motor model with back EMF, torque, heat losses and temperature-dependent resistance.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Current, angular speed and temperature interact:\nback EMF and torque couple winding and rotor, while ohmic heating\nchanges the temperature-dependent resistance.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>Multiplying the first equation by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>i<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe second by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ω<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand adding the thermal equation yields the energy balance from L1. The\nback EMF and driving torque exchange power between domains, and ohmic\nlosses become heat. This identity provides an independent invariant\ncheck for the subsequent computation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Nonlinear state space and operating point<\/h2>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">𝖳<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=(i,\\omega,\\theta)^\\mathsf T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">𝖳<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=(u,M_L)^\\mathsf T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot x=f(x,v)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nan output\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=g(x,v)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncould be the rotational speed, for example. The products\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>i<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmake the model nonlinear. Superposition therefore does not apply to\narbitrary operating states.<\/p>\n<p>A steady state\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_*<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsatisfies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x_*,v_*)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a constant input and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>G<\/mi><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msubsup><mi>i<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_*=(ki_*-M_L)/b,\\qquad G\\theta_*=R(\\theta_*)i_*^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The electrical equation closes the system. If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ntemperature is decoupled in the sense that it has no feedback effect,\nand the steady-state values can be determined directly:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>k<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msubsup><mi>i<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>/<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i_*=\\frac{u+kM_L/b}{R_0+k^2/b},\\qquad \\theta_*=R_0i_*^2/G.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>24<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=24<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M_L=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nNm,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_0=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nΩ,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.02<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=0.02<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nW/K, we obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>7.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i_*=7.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nA,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>47.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_*=47.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrad/s and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>52.5625<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_*=52.5625<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nK. The input power of 174 W splits into 23.75 W of load power, 45.125 W\nof frictional power and 105.125 W of heat dissipation. The model with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha\\ne0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nleads to different operating points.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Linearisation for small deviations<\/h2>\n<p>For a fixed operating point, approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta\\dot x=A\\delta x+B\\delta v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith the derivatives of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nevaluated at that point. Write\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_*=R(\\theta_*)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>α<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R&#39;=R_0\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>k<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>J<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>J<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><msub><mi>i<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><msubsup><mi>i<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>−<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>J<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\begin{pmatrix}\n-R_*/L &amp; -k/L &amp; -R&#39;i_*/L\\\\\nk/J &amp; -b/J &amp; 0\\\\\n2R_*i_*/C &amp; 0 &amp; (R&#39;i_*^2-G)/C\n\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad\nB=\\begin{pmatrix}1/L&amp;0\\\\0&amp;-1/J\\\\0&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For speed measurement alone,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta y=(0,1,0)\\delta x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe eigenvalues of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nprovide information about local dynamics; negative real parts of all\neigenvalues imply local asymptotic stability of a smooth autonomous\nmodel at the operating point. The statement is not automatically global\nand does not apply unchanged at a switching instant in a hybrid\nsystem.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Timescales and choice of coupling<\/h2>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nH,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.02<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J=0.02<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkg m² and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=40<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nJ/K, the uncoupled indicative values are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.05<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L/R_0=0.05<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J/b=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C/G=20<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns. The actual modes are coupled. A long-term thermal model could replace\nthe fast states with algebraic steady-state relationships, provided the\nload and supply vary slowly enough and start-up currents are not of\ninterest. Depending on the formulation, this produces a reduced or\ndifferential-algebraic model. Such a reduction is unsuitable for\nstart-up; the inductance enforces a continuous current under finite\nvoltage.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the initial rate of increase of current at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>24<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=24<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nV,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i=\\omega=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nH?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>i<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>240<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot i=240<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nA/s.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Determine\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_0=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nΩ,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.004<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0.004<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nK⁻¹ and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta=50<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nK.<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> 2.4 Ω.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does rising temperature immediately affect the current\nderivative when current is positive?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> The partial derivative\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mi>i<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-R&#39;i/L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis negative; over the longer term, speed and back EMF also change.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Further reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Numerical\nintegration<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Case\nstudy<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://doc.modelica.org/Modelica%204.0.0/Resources/helpDymola/Modelica_Electrical_Machines_BasicMachines_DCMachines.html\">Modelica\nStandard Library 4.0.0 – DCMachines<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · DC_PermanentMagnet,\nInformation and Parameters: armature resistance, inductance,\ntemperature-dependent losses and model limitations; version 4.0.0. ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://doc.modelica.org/Modelica%204.0.0/Resources/helpWSM/Modelica/Modelica.Electrical.Machines.Examples.DCMachines.DCPM_Cooling.html\">Modelica\n– Motor cooling example<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Information: DC motor with cooling circuit\nand thermal capacities; model example. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M12-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M12-L3 – Numerical Integration and Convergence<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M12 – Bachelor: Modelling and Simulation of Complex Systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Differential\nequations<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Computational\nexperiments<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">State\nspace<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Implement Euler’s method and\nclassical RK4, determine error orders experimentally, and distinguish\nnumerical stability from physical stability.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Discretisation of an initial-value problem<\/h2>\n<p>We seek the solution of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot x=f(t,x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(t_0)=x_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith step size\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nset\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_n=t_0+nh<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand calculate approximations\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe explicit Euler method uses the slope at the start:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{n+1}=x_n+h f(t_n,x_n).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>It follows from the first-order Taylor expansion. For sufficiently\nsmooth solutions and stable error propagation, the local step error is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>h<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(h^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the global error at a fixed final time is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(h)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe formula applies to vectors as well as scalars. A step is not\nnecessarily a physical sampling operation; it may serve only for\nnumerical integration.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Classical fourth-order Runge–Kutta method<\/h2>\n<p>The four slopes are<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"right\" class=\"text-right\"><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>=<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{aligned}\nk_1&amp;=f(t_n,x_n),\\\\\nk_2&amp;=f(t_n+h/2,x_n+hk_1/2),\\\\\nk_3&amp;=f(t_n+h/2,x_n+hk_2/2),\\\\\nk_4&amp;=f(t_n+h,x_n+hk_3).\n\\end{aligned}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mi>h<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{n+1}=x_n+\\frac h6(k_1+2k_2+2k_3+k_4).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>With sufficient smoothness and suitable stability, the global error\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>h<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(h^4)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nat the cost of four function evaluations per step. Discontinuities in\ninputs or model equations must be handled separately; otherwise the\nexpected order may be lost. Events are the subject of <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M12-L4<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Reference experiment: exponential decay<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot x=-2x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(0)=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe exact solution is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(t)=e^{-2t}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWe compare the absolute final error at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns. The observed order for two step sizes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=\\log_2(e_h/e_{h/2})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nprovided neither error is zero. The following code requires only the\nPython standard library.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> math <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> exp, log2<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> integrate(h, rk):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    x <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">1.0<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> _ <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span>h)):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        k1 <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>x<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> rk:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            k2 <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(x<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>h<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>k1<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            k3 <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(x<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>h<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>k2<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            k4 <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(x<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>h<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>k3)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            x <span class=\"op\">+=<\/span> h<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(k1<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>k2<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>k3<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>k4)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">6<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            x <span class=\"op\">+=<\/span> h<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>k1<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(x<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>exp(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>last <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"va\">None<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> h <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> (<span class=\"fl\">.2<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">.1<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">.05<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">.025<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-18\"><a href=\"#cb1-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    err <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [integrate(h, rk) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> rk <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> (<span class=\"va\">False<\/span>, <span class=\"va\">True<\/span>)]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-19\"><a href=\"#cb1-19\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    order <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [] <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> last <span class=\"kw\">is<\/span> <span class=\"va\">None<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span> [log2(a<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>b) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> a,b <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">zip<\/span>(last,err)]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-20\"><a href=\"#cb1-20\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(h, err, order)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-21\"><a href=\"#cb1-21\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    last <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> err<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th class=\"text-right\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(s)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Euler final error<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">RK4 final error<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.2<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">5.75753 · 10⁻²<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">8.07314 · 10⁻⁵<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2.79611 · 10⁻²<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">4.26519 · 10⁻⁶<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.05<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1.37586 · 10⁻²<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2.45185 · 10⁻⁷<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.025<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">6.82313 · 10⁻³<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1.46976 · 10⁻⁸<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_039_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Calculated final errors for Euler and RK4 on logarithmic axes.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: End error decreases as the step size shrinks for\nboth methods; on the log-log plot, RK4 falls much more steeply than\nEuler over the range investigated.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>The last halving yields an observed order of about 1.01 for Euler and\nabout 4.06 for RK4. The theoretical values of 1 and 4 apply\nasymptotically; for larger steps, the ratios may differ. With very small\nsteps, rounding errors may dominate.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Stability limits and stiff systems<\/h2>\n<p>For the test problem\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot x=\\lambda x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith real\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda&lt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nEuler yields the amplification factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+h\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDecay requires\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|1+h\\lambda|&lt;1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;h&lt;2/|\\lambda|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=-2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns⁻¹ and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=1.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns, the result is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nalternating signs and increasing magnitude, although the exact solution\nremains positive and decreases. RK4 has the factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>z<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>z<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>z<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>24<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+z+z^2/2+z^3/6+z^4/24<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=h\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\non the negative real axis its stability interval extends approximately\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2.785<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=-2.785<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>When timescales differ greatly, an explicit method may require very\nsmall steps even though only slow results are of interest. This is a\ntypical situation for stiff problems. Implicit methods such as backward\nEuler or BDF, and adaptive step-size control, are possible alternatives.\nAn adaptive solver controls a numerical error estimate using absolute\nand relative tolerances; it does not verify whether the physical\nparameters are correct. For states with different scales, component-wise\ntolerances are useful.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Euler with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=-10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns⁻¹ and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=0.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns: stable?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> No, amplification factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">An RK4 result has an error smaller by a factor of 16 when the step\nsize is halved. What order does this indicate?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>16<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\log_2(16)=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is a result obtained with two almost identical step sizes not\nyet physical evidence?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> It indicates numerical consistency, but alone\nsays nothing about model assumptions, parameters or measurement\ndata.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Further reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Hybrid\nmodels<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Model\nvalidation<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://persson.berkeley.edu/Programming_for_Mathematical_Applications/content/Differential_Equations/Chapter_Intro.html\">Persson/Franco/Sweeney\nBlanco – Programming for Mathematical Applications, Chapter 14<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nChapter 14, introduction: initial value problems, Euler and RK4;\nfurther-reading overview, subsection 14.1 not retrieved. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.integrate.solve_ivp.html\">SciPy\n– solve_ivp: Methods and tolerances<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · solve_ivp function\ndescription: numerical solution of an initial value problem. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M12-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M12-L4 – Discrete Events and Hybrid Models<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M12 – Bachelor: Modelling and Simulation of Complex Systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">State\nmachines<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Temperature\ncontrol<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Numerical\nintegration<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Combine the continuous states\nand discrete modes of a system and determine the error associated with\ndiscrete-time switching detection.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Continuous Dynamics and Discrete Logic<\/h2>\n<p>A hybrid model combines real-valued states\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand discrete modes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q\\in Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBetween events,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot x=f_q(t,x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\napplies; a transition is triggered by a condition (guard). During a\ntransition, the mode and, where physically justified, the states can\nchange. A reset\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^+=r(x^-)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis useful, for example, in an idealised impact involving an abrupt\nchange in velocity. With finite heating power, however, temperature\ncannot jump.<\/p>\n<p>A time event occurs at a known time, such as a scheduled load step.\nIn a state event, a threshold is reached during integration. A\nsampled-data controller, by contrast, is designed to make decisions only\nat sampling instants. These three cases must not be confused during\nimplementation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Thermostat with Hysteresis<\/h2>\n<p>A heater with power\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nW supplies a lumped thermal mass\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nJ/K. The thermal conductance to the surroundings is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nW/K. The temperature rise\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta=T-T_a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfollows<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mover><mi>θ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>q<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C\\dot\\theta=qP-G\\theta,\\qquad q\\in\\{0,1\\}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta\\le2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nK, the heater is switched on; at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta\\ge4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nK, it is switched off. Between these thresholds, the preceding mode is\nretained. Initially,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nK and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor ideally smooth signals, hysteresis prevents immediate back-and-forth\nswitching at a single threshold. Real sensors may nevertheless require\nfiltering or additional minimum switching intervals.<\/p>\n<p>For a constant mode, with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=C/G=10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta_\\infty=qP/G<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe exact solution is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta(t)=\\theta_\\infty+(\\theta_0-\\theta_\\infty)e^{-t/\\tau}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The heating phase from 2 to 4 K lasts\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.87682<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10\\ln(8/6)=2.87682<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns, and the cooling phase from 4 to 2 K lasts\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6.93147<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10\\ln(4/2)=6.93147<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns. The period is 9.80829 s. The heating duty cycle is approximately\n29.33%, and the mean heating power is about 2.93 W. Since the state is\nidentical after a full period, the mean heat dissipation equals the mean\nheating power.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_040_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Temperature trajectory and switching state of an ideal thermostat with hysteresis.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Temperature and the discrete heater switch jointly\ndescribe the thermostat state; distinct on and off thresholds prevent\nimmediate switching back and forth.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Stepwise versus Event-Accurate Switching<\/h2>\n<p>Even exact integration within a step detects the threshold event too\nlate if the condition is checked only at the end of the step. The\nfollowing comparison calculates the first switch-off time; the\ntemperature trajectory between sampling instants is analytically\nexact.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> math <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> exp, log, ceil<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>exact <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">10<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>log(<span class=\"dv\">8<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> h <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> (<span class=\"fl\">1.0<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">.25<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">.1<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    n <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> ceil(exact<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>h)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    t <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> n<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>h<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    theta <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">10<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">8<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>exp(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>t<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(h, t, theta, t<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>exact)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Checking interval (s)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">First switch-off (s)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Temperature rise at switch-off (K)<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1.00<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">3.00<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">4.07345<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.50<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">3.00<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">4.07345<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.25<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">3.00<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">4.07345<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.10<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2.90<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">4.01389<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>For an ideal continuous-time thermostat, the root of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(\\theta)=\\theta-4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmust be located in heating mode. For general models, the root of the\ncontinuous approximation can be located using interpolation or\nbisection. At the event, integration proceeds up to the threshold, the\nmode changes, and the remainder of the time step is calculated in the\nnew mode. For cooling mode, the relevant function is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(\\theta)=\\theta-2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith a downward crossing direction. Solvers that only look for sign\nchanges within each step can miss multiple crossings in the same step;\nan appropriate maximum step size and event logic are necessary.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Robust Mode Logic<\/h2>\n<p>An algorithm requires an unambiguous initial mode and consistent\nhandling of simultaneous events. In the thermostat, the state is\ncontinuous; only\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchanges abruptly. For known time events, the integration interval is\nsplit exactly at the specified time. In <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M12-L5<\/a>,\nload and voltage steps are handled in this way; the right-hand side\nremains smooth within each interval. Ever smaller step sizes are no\nsubstitute for properly formulated event logic.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with Solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nK, what is the mode?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> It cannot be determined without knowing the\npreceding mode; hysteresis has discrete memory.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does the cooling time change when the thermal capacity is\ndoubled?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> With unchanged thermal conductance, it doubles\nto 13.86294 s.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which states of the motor from M12-L2 jump in response to a finite\nvoltage step?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> None of the three stored states; their\nderivatives may change. An algebraic output can jump immediately.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Further reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Parameters,\nsensitivity and model validation<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://mbe.modelica.university/behavior/discrete/\">Modelica by\nExample – Discrete Behavior<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter introduction Discrete\nBehavior: discrete behavior versus continuous models. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://specification.modelica.org/maint/3.6/equations.html\">Modelica\n3.6 – Equations and Events<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter 8, §8.3.5: when-equations;\npartial scope. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.integrate.solve_ivp.html\">SciPy\n– solve_ivp: event functions<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Parameters events, terminal and\ndirection: event detection and termination; partial scope. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_core_en:M12-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M12-L5 – Parameters, Sensitivity and Model Validation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M12 – Bachelor: Modelling and Simulation of Complex Systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Coupled\nmodel<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Integration<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Events<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Measurement\nuncertainty<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Verify an electrothermal\nmulti-component model using two step sizes, compare two model variants\nagainst synthetic reference data and quantify parameter sensitivity.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Research Question and Test Criteria<\/h2>\n<p>We ask whether temperature-dependent winding resistance is necessary\nto predict the speed and temperature of an idealised drive over 120 s.\nThe equations are taken from <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M12-L2<\/a>.\nThe initial conditions are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i=\\omega=\\theta=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith an ambient temperature of 20 °C. This is purely a simulation\nexample, with no real machine or measured values. The parameters are\neducational model values, not a design or operating approval.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Parameter<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Value<\/th>\n<th>Unit<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<td>Ω<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.1<\/td>\n<td>H<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>J<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.02<\/td>\n<td>kg m²<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.02<\/td>\n<td>Nm s/rad<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.2<\/td>\n<td>Nm/A (SI-consistent back-EMF constant)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">40<\/td>\n<td>J/K<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>G<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<td>W/K<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.004<\/td>\n<td>K⁻¹<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Time interval (s)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Voltage (V)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Load torque (Nm)<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0 to 40<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">24<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.5<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>40 to 80<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">24<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1.0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>80 to 120<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">18<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.5<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>Input steps are treated as segment boundaries. The three states\nremain continuous. The solution of the same nonlinear model using a\ndifferent integration method (DOP853, strict tolerances) serves as the\nsynthetic reference. This enables practice in numerical and model\ncomparison; since the reference comes from a model rather than\nmeasurements, this is not empirical validation. For the exercise, limits\nof 0.2 rad/s for speed RMSE and 1 K for temperature RMSE are specified\nbefore comparison. For the numerical calculation, halving the step size\nmust result in endpoint differences of at most 0.001 A, 0.001 rad/s and\n0.001 K, as well as a maximum energy-balance residual below 0.01 J.\nThese tolerances apply only to this exercise.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Reproducible Calculation<\/h2>\n<p>The additional state\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nintegrates the net boundary power\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>M<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><msup><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ui-M_L\\omega-b\\omega^2-G\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt does not influence the other three equations. Thus, the\nenergy-balance residual\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><msup><mi>i<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>J<\/mi><msup><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Li^2/2+J\\omega^2/2+C\\theta-E_B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis evaluated at every calculation point (zero initial energy). The code\nuses Python, NumPy and SciPy. Both step sizes align exactly with the\nsegment boundaries and reference times.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> scipy.integrate <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> solve_ivp<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"co\"># Zustand: i [A], omega [rad/s], theta [K], Nettoenergie [J].<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>R0, L, J, b, k, C <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">.1<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">.02<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">.02<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">.2<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">40.<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>segments <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">40<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">24.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>), (<span class=\"dv\">40<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">80<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">24.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>), (<span class=\"dv\">80<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">120<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">18.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>)]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> rhs(x, u, load, alpha, G):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    i, w, th, E <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> x<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    R <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> R0<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span>alpha<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>th)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> np.array([(u<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>R<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>i<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>k<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>w)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>L,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>                     (k<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>i<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>b<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>w<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>load)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>J,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>                     (R<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>i<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>i<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>G<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>th)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>C,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>                     u<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>i<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>load<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>w<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>b<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>w<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>w<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>G<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>th])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> rk4(h, alpha<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.004<\/span>, G<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    x <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.zeros(<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-18\"><a href=\"#cb1-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    out <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [x.copy()]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-19\"><a href=\"#cb1-19\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> a, z, u, load <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> segments:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-20\"><a href=\"#cb1-20\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        f <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">lambda<\/span> x: rhs(x, u, load, alpha, G)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-21\"><a href=\"#cb1-21\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> n <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>((z<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>a)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>h)):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-22\"><a href=\"#cb1-22\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            q1 <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> f(x)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> q2 <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> f(x<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>h<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>q1<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-23\"><a href=\"#cb1-23\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            q3 <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> f(x<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>h<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>q2<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> q4 <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> f(x<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>h<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>q3)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-24\"><a href=\"#cb1-24\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            x <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> x<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>h<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(q1<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>q2<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>q3<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>q4)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">6<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-25\"><a href=\"#cb1-25\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            out.append(x.copy())<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-26\"><a href=\"#cb1-26\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> np.array(out)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-27\"><a href=\"#cb1-27\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-28\"><a href=\"#cb1-28\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> reference():<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-29\"><a href=\"#cb1-29\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    x <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.zeros(<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-30\"><a href=\"#cb1-30\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    rows <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [x.copy()]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-31\"><a href=\"#cb1-31\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> a,z,u,load <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> segments:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-32\"><a href=\"#cb1-32\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        sol <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> solve_ivp(<span class=\"kw\">lambda<\/span> t,x: rhs(x,u,load,<span class=\"fl\">.004<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>),<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-33\"><a href=\"#cb1-33\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>                        (a,z), x, method<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"st\">&#39;DOP853&#39;<\/span>, rtol<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1e-11<\/span>,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-34\"><a href=\"#cb1-34\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>                        atol<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1e-12<\/span>, t_eval<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.arange(a<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>,z<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-35\"><a href=\"#cb1-35\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> sol.success<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-36\"><a href=\"#cb1-36\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        rows.extend(sol.y.T)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-37\"><a href=\"#cb1-37\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        x <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> sol.y[:,<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-38\"><a href=\"#cb1-38\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> np.array(rows)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-39\"><a href=\"#cb1-39\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-40\"><a href=\"#cb1-40\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> balance(x):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-41\"><a href=\"#cb1-41\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>L<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>x[:,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>J<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>x[:,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span>C<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>x[:,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>x[:,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-42\"><a href=\"#cb1-42\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-43\"><a href=\"#cb1-43\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> <span class=\"va\">__name__<\/span> <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&#39;__main__&#39;<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-44\"><a href=\"#cb1-44\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    ref <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> reference()<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-45\"><a href=\"#cb1-45\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> t,r <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">zip<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">121<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>),ref): <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(t,<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>[<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(v,<span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> v <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> r[:<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>]])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-46\"><a href=\"#cb1-46\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> h <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> [<span class=\"fl\">.02<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.01<\/span>]:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-47\"><a href=\"#cb1-47\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> alpha,G <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> [(<span class=\"fl\">.004<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>),(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>),(<span class=\"fl\">.004<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">1.8<\/span>),(<span class=\"fl\">.004<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">2.2<\/span>)]:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-48\"><a href=\"#cb1-48\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            x<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>rk4(h,alpha,G)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> y<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>x[::<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span>h)]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-49\"><a href=\"#cb1-49\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(h,alpha,G,<span class=\"st\">&#39;end&#39;<\/span>,x[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,:<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>], <span class=\"st\">&#39;rmse&#39;<\/span>,np.sqrt(np.mean((y[:,:<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>ref[:,:<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,axis<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>)), <span class=\"st\">&#39;max energy residual&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(balance(x))), <span class=\"st\">&#39;peak temp&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(x[:,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>]))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Reference Values and Verification<\/h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Time (s)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Current (A)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Speed (rad/s)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Temperature rise (K)<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.000<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.000<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.000<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">10<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">6.938<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">44.590<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">21.761<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">20<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">6.786<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">42.979<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">33.776<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">30<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">6.697<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">42.041<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">41.040<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">40<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">6.644<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">41.486<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">45.426<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">50<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">7.648<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">26.595<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">55.337<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">60<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">7.564<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">25.709<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">61.648<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">70<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">7.515<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">25.186<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">65.439<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">80<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">7.485<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">24.875<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">67.715<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">90<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">5.194<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">26.838<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">53.995<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">100<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">5.272<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">27.652<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">45.672<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">110<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">5.319<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">28.156<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">40.640<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">120<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">5.349<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">28.465<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">37.597<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The table is rounded for presentation; the code compares against\nunrounded reference values. For the nonlinear model, RK4 produces the\nfollowing metrics:<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-quarter\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Step size (s)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Final speed (rad/s)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Final temperature rise (K)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Max. energy-balance residual (J)<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.02<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">28.46533816<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">37.59736237<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.0056041<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.01<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">28.46533835<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">37.59736046<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.0003080<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The final current also differs by only about 2 · 10⁻⁸ A. The criteria\nare satisfied. The energy check is complementary, not sufficient: even\nan incorrect resistance model can conserve energy. Small differences at\nthe endpoint could conceal errors in the trajectory, so reference values\nare additionally checked every 10 s and the maximum balance deviation\nacross all steps is considered.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model Comparison and Sensitivity<\/h2>\n<p>The root mean square error (RMSE) for an output\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>13<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=13<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nreference instants is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">RMSE<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>N<\/mi><\/mfrac><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>N<\/mi><\/munderover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{RMSE}(y)=\\sqrt{\\frac1N\\sum_{j=1}^N(y_j-y_{\\mathrm{ref},j})^2}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The full model, a simplified model with constant resistance\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>),\nand two variants of the thermal conductance are compared. All use\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.01<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=0.01<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ns and the same input trajectory.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-20\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-26\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-26\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-26\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Variant<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Speed RMSE (rad/s)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Temperature RMSE (K)<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Maximum temperature rise (K,\ncomputational grid)<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Full model<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.00000020<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.00000148<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">67.715<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">6.45331<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.47226<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">68.584<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G=1.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nW/K<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.51530<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">4.51427<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">73.950<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G=2.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nW/K<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0.44917<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">3.83290<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">62.367<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/U_ELG_041_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Synthetic motor reference and predictions with constant and temperature-dependent winding resistance.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Synthetic reference traces for speed and temperature\nare compared with two resistance models; matching temperature alone does\nnot establish model validity.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p>The constant-resistance model meets the temperature criterion but\nfalls well short of the speed criterion. A good prediction for a single\noutput is not sufficient. At 120 s, its speed is 32.5 rad/s rather than\n28.465 rad/s. In the full model, the maximum winding temperature is\nabout 87.7 °C (20 °C ambient temperature plus the temperature rise). The\nmaxima are determined on the computational grid and are not precisely\nlocated extrema.<\/p>\n<p>For sensitivity with respect to the thermal conductance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>G<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na centred difference quotient can be used. From the two variants, the\nmaximum temperature rise yields approximately<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>≈<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>62.367<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>73.950<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mn>2.2<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1.8<\/mn><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>28.96<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mfrac><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\partial\\theta_\\mathrm{max}}{\\partial G}\\approx\\frac{62.367-73.950}{2.2-1.8}=-28.96\\,\\frac{\\mathrm K^2}{\\mathrm W}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The normalised sensitivity at the nominal value is approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>28.96<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>67.715<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.855<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-28.96)\\cdot2/67.715=-0.855<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe spread represents a local single-parameter investigation, not a\nprobability distribution or a prediction interval for a real system. The\n±10% is a chosen variation, not measured uncertainty. Nonlinearity is\nevident in the fact that the two temperature changes are not precisely\nsymmetrical.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Verification, Calibration and Empirical Validation<\/h2>\n<p>Verification checks whether the model equations have been implemented\ncorrectly and solved with sufficient accuracy. Calibration estimates\nunknown model parameters using data. Empirical validation assesses the\nsuitability of the model for its intended purpose using appropriate real\nreference data, preferably independent of the data used for fitting. A\nreal investigation would require voltage, current, speed and temperature\nmeasurements with measurement uncertainty, a documented load profile and\nseparate calibration and test runs. The sensor position and the meaning\nof a single winding temperature would need clarification. If only\ntemperature were known, the error of the constant-resistance model might\nnot be visible. Accordingly, the choice of measured quantities must\nmatch the actual decision question.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises with Solutions<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can the two temperature-calculation variants not be made\nidentical by halving the step size again?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> The deviation caused by changing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>G<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a parameter or model difference, not a discretisation error.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">By how many kelvins does the temperature maximum increase at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G=1.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrather than 2 W/K?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> About 6.235 K.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Is the constant-resistance model acceptable for a temperature RMSE\nalone below 1 K in this example?<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> Yes, for this single criterion in the\nsynthetic reference case. This does not establish acceptability for\nspeed or other operating profiles.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Design an additional reference run.<br />\n<strong>Solution<\/strong> For example, an independent load profile with\nseveral temperature measurement points and measured speed; define error\nlimits in advance to suit the intended use, and do not readjust\nparameters using the test run.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Further reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering-core&amp;page=study-path&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Study\npathway<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://standards.nasa.gov/sites/default/files/standards/NASA/B/1/NASA-STD-7009B-Final-3-5-2024.pdf\">NASA\n– NASA-STD-7009B, Standard for Models and Simulations (PDF)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nDefinitions, Domain of Verification and Empirical Validation; §\n4.2.5.2–4.2.6: empirical validation and reference data. · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://standards.nasa.gov/node/263\">NASA – NASA-STD-7009B,\nStandard for Models and Simulations<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Document Scope: credibility,\nmodel life cycle and acceptance criteria; standard overview only, not\nfull-text evidence. · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.integrate.solve_ivp.html\">SciPy\n– solve_ivp<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · solve_ivp function description and method, rtol, atol\nparameters: numerical integration and comparison of solvers. · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
